import
	../../html

intro-specs html[]()
	- section "Type parameters are opaque",
		- p quote
			Normally, you can't do anything with a type parameter {t}
			unless it would work for every type.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log all-same 1, 1, 1

			all-same[t] bool(a t, b t, c t)
				# This won't work
				a == b and b == c
		- p quote
			The above example doesn't work even though there is an {fn-equal} function for {nat}s.
			{br}
			{all-same} can't be defined that way unless {fn-equal} worked for every type (which it doesn't).

		- p "This can be fixed by declaring {all-same} to use the {spec-equal} spec."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log all-same 1, 1, 1
				log all-same 1, 1, 2

			all-same[t] bool(a t, b t, c t) t equal
				a == b and b == c

		- p quote
			The above example works because the {spec-equal} spec predeclares that an {fn-equal} function should exist.
			{br}
			Then the {em "caller"} of {fn-foo} is responsible for providing that function.
		- p quote
			The caller knows the actual type substituted for {t} (in this case {nat})
			and looks for a concrete {fn-equal} function.
			{br}
			In this case it looks for {fn-equal}{sp}{bool}{lparen}{var-a}{sp}{nat}{comma}{var-b}{sp}{nat}{rparen}
			and finds it in the standard library.

		- aside-small quote
			Like types, {spec_}s have a suffix syntax: {type "t equal"} uses the {spec-equal} spec
			with {t} as its type argument.

		- p quote
			Specs are written after function parameters.
			{br}
			Multiple specs can be separated by commas as in {t}{sp}{type "data"}{comma}{t}{sp}{spec-equal} .
			{br}
			For a multi-line function signature, specs are written after the {do_} keyword.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log all-same 1, 1, 1
				log all-same 1, 1, 2

			all-same[t] bool
				a t
				b t
				c t
			do t equal
				a == b and b == c

	- section "Defining specs",
		- p "A {spec_} declaration has a list of signatures in an indented block."

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/math/math

			main void()
				log prism-volume (circle 1), 2
				log prism-volume (square 1), 7

			area[t] spec
				area float(a t)

			prism-volume[t] float(a t, depth float) t area
				depth * area a

			circle record(radius float)
			area float(a circle)
				pi * a.radius ** 2

			square record(width float)
			area float(a square)
				a.width ** 2

		- p quote
			This defines an {type "area"} spec and uses it to define {function "prism-volume"}.
			{br}
			That allows that function to call {function "area"} on the type parameter.

		- p "The syntax for spec signatures is the same as for union methods, but there is no implicit first parameter."

	- section "Spec inheritance",
		- p "A spec can inherit from other specs."

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/math/math

			main void()
				log area-over-perimeter circle 1
				log area-over-perimeter square 1

			area-over-perimeter[t] float(a t) t shape
				a.area / a.perimeter

			shape[t] spec t area
				perimeter float(a t)

			area[t] spec
				area float(a t)

			circle record(radius float)
			area float(a circle)
				pi * a.radius ** 2
			perimeter float(a circle)
				pi * a.radius * 2

			square record(width float)
			area float(a square)
				a.width ** 2
			perimeter float(a square)
				a.width * 4

		- p quote
			In the above example, {type "shape"} inherits from {type "area"},
			so it includes both {function "area"} and {function "perimeter"} signatures.

	- section "Special specs",
		- p quote
			The special spec {spec-data} doesn't include any signatures.
			Instead, it is only satisfied for {data_} types.
			(See {intro-link mutability}.)
			{br}
			{spec-shared} is the corresponding spec for {shared_} types.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				foo 1
				foo ()::nat mut[] # won't work

			foo[t] void(_ t) t data
				()

		- p quote
			There are also two special specs {global_} and {unsafe} that don't have a type argument or any signatures.
			See {intro-link globals} and {intro-link unsafe}.

	- section "Implicit functions and specs",
		- p quote
			Implicit functions like {fn-equal} will use specs as necessary.
			{br}
			Below, the implicit {fn-equal} for {type "wrapper"} needs to use the spec {t}{sp}{spec-equal}
			so that it can call {fn-equal} on {variable "value"}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat wrapper = value: 1
				log a == a

			wrapper[t] record(value t)

	- section "Multiple type parameters",
		- p quote
			Specs can have any number of type parameters.
			(Even 0 which is useful for avoiding circular imports. See {intro-link modules}.)
			{br}
			The below uses the {type "to"} spec, which takes the return type and parameter type of a
			{fn-to} function as type parameters.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log next a
				log next b
				log next c

			e enum(a, b, c)

			next[t] t?(a t) (nat, t) to, (t?, nat) to
				to::t? a.to::nat + 1

	- section "Methods and specs",
		- p "Methods (and method implementations) can not declare specs."

-spec-equal html-node()
	type "equal"

-all-same html-node()
	function "all-same"
